what i learned from doing 20 episodes of  this demo rpg series and teaching a  little bit of c plus  i've definitely learned some things some  things i did not anticipate learning in  fact  one of the main reasons there's two main  reasons i did this series i'll just  start with those  the first one was basically to have some  consistent content in a new series come  out  i think every channel needs that those  seem to be what have grown my channel  mostly in the past and if you're a new  youtuber i would highly advise making  some sort of series it almost  doesn't matter what it is just anything  you feel a little bit passionate about  why not make a series on it that way you  have some continued content and things  for people to keep coming back to so  that was one of the reasons but there  were other reasons i guess the other  main reason was i really wanted to just  make a library like this and i always  have there are more than two reasons now  i'm thinking about it i've always wanted  to make this sort of library just for  the fun of it really i just always  thought it would be fun to do this  and so i was kind of killing two birds  with one stone there i  well even more  because i also wanted to find a way to  teach c plus plus that wasn't just going  through a list of here the different  things you can do i wanted an actual  project so that would be the third thing  so yeah having a consistent series i've  always wanted to make a library of this  rpg stuff  and  just being able to teach c plus plus in  a way that was a little  different those were my main aspirations  here  and i settled on doing 20 episodes so  what did i learn in these 20 episodes oh  boy i learned a lot a lot more than i  thought i would we started on january  24th that's when the first one was  released i figured it would take about a  month and we did the last episode on  march 6th so in episode one i learned  that uh you need to zoom in people can't  just magically read tiny text there were  a lot of comments about i can't read  anything but overall i was just kind of  spitting the idea i had and people  seemed to  resonate with that so that was kind of  cool i wasn't sure if people would or  not  and in episode two and three i was  starting to get into adding some some  classes and levels and  just some general work and i started to  realize that there were a lot of people  who were aware of c plus plus stuff that  i wasn't which is  totally expected i guess in some way or  another but i wasn't expecting to run  into  things where i needed to go do research  i i kind of went into this thinking i  know how to do all this already but  you'll always be surprised there's  always more to learn with c plus plus  and that was one of the things there was  a some comments about  use composition over  inheritance and i was like okay maybe i  need to look into this more because i  seem to be using inheritance a bit much  and they were right so in episode 4 i uh  corrected that to some extent and tried  to use it better going forward but there  were definitely some important lessons  there about that and it really made me  kind of rethink my design entirely and i  actually had to take a little time off  let's see what there were two days in  between these commits so it took like a  couple days i was planning on doing like  an episode a day but there were some  times when i needed a couple days  and it looks like between episode 8 and  9 i took more  but also some other things started to  come up here and i started to realize  that there is just going to be people  that aren't going to be happy no matter  what i do creative decisions are often  roadblocks and sometimes you just got to  make a decision  and go forward and try to document it  maybe leave a comment or two so that was  like the big takeaways was basically to  not get stuck in creative decisions  and don't worry about what people are  gonna think or say because  they might be right for them but they're  not necessarily right for you and  sometimes people are just like  bitter and annoyed and leave comments as  so i kind of just i kind of started to  see around 11 12 i started thinking  what if this stuff doesn't work right  and that's where i realized i really  need tests and i really need to make  this a library that i can test that  seemed to be pretty critical and i've  also realized when i started doing this  that  a lot of programmers  just totally skipped the test phase and  it's understandable why especially in  game development usually usually your  game is the test  however  when you're playing your game you're  rarely testing your edge cases and when  they do come up if you haven't handled  them you're going to get a crash or some  weird thing is going to happen in your  game and that's that's actually quite  critical so you should make tests no  matter what  and that's a really important takeaway  that i think more people need to  embrace i'd say there's just there's  just so many times when people uh are  making stuff and  and just want to get it done so they  skip over the test i'm trying to think  of a great analogy but there really  isn't one you just have to have tests  it's it's way more important than you  realize especially with critical  applications if you're just coding for  fun it's not so big of a deal  but it makes people take your program a  lot more seriously and i made a whole  video on that so you can check that out  if you want  now there's somewhere in here in like  between february 22nd to february 24th  where i basically  took time off the series and just  refactored things and  made a bunch of tests  and that was a pretty critical moment  because well it took a lot of time but  there wasn't a whole lot to teach other  than you should be designing first if  you want to start a new project  you can't just sit down and code well  you can and that's what i did with this  i literally just sat down and started  coding  with just the idea of i want to make an  rpg i didn't really have a big plan  nothing sketched out or anything like  that and i tend to operate like that but  that's just me  most people can't operate like that i go  in i i kind of know the end goal of  where i want to get  to but  the step-by-step little minute things i  kind of just make up as i go what the  heck is my green screen dude stop  stop  so  yeah i think in the future i'm going to  try to plan a little better however you  can't always plan there's always little  things that come up so i think keeping a  notepad handy  just one of these anything and just  jotting down any little things you know  you need to work out making things to  check off seems to be helpful but i can  never maintain that do any of you guys  have the same problem like i go to start  making a list and i'll start jotting  things down and i'll stick with it for  like one day and then the next day i  just i don't care about my list anymore  i'm just gonna go to work episode 15 and  on this is where things started becoming  different for the series this is  basically where i started implementing  the library as an actual piece of  software as an actual program that you  could run basically i put in monsters  and battles first and turn it into sort  of a battle arena and started making  items drop and making sure you could use  items and then use abilities and then we  basically ran out of time we got the  episode 20 that's as far as we were  planning to go for this series but the  whole point of the series was that  by going this far and taking all these  little steps you'll learn all kinds of  new things and learn how you can do it  on your own learn how you can expand it  on your own memory management is really  important and that you should probably  be using shared and unique pointers  wherever you can  because there were a lot of these  episodes where i was spending a lot of  time just wrapping my head around what  was happening with the data and the  pointers and that has definitely made a  lot easier with unique pointers although  there's a whole learning curve to those  as well but i guess that was the main  takeaway is  yeah design the library with  the memory management in mind of how you  want to use it and that there is a lot  of different ways you might want to use  it in general for this we wanted to  there were some really important things  like we want to make sure you can't dupe  items we want to make sure  that uh  there aren't multiple references to the  same item being owned and things like  that  basically because we wanted to reduce  the amount of bugs in the game where you  could cheat and just like you know do  random stuff like dupe items or get  way more of something than you should  have so there was a lot of that in mind  and that was the big takeaways i think  for the final episodes was just  carefully managing uh your resources  and your loops uh yeah your loops are  super important in a game too  when you look at it from like a bird's  eye perspective and it's just a bunch of  while loops here and there and then  charts all over the place it's really  hard to grasp but when you dig in and  just go step by step it's not so bad  because most mainly there's one main  while loop and in that while loop  occasionally you get in other little  loops if you're doing this for a while  or you're doing that for a while but  you're still in this core  big one  so yeah i guess that's it  you
 

